Snowflake and data generation rules for Crocevia project

1) Source tables and stages
- Use RAW for external/raw inputs: CROCEVIA_DB.RAW_DATA
- Quarterly sales table: CROCEVIA_DB.RAW_DATA.CARREFOUR_FRANCE_QUARTERLY_SALES
- Stock price table: CROCEVIA_DB.RAW_DATA.CARREFOUR_STOCKPRICE
- Stage used for Python handlers: CROCEVIA_DB.RAW_DATA.CR_BRONZE_STAGE
- Bronze operational tables live in CROCEVIA_DB.BRONZE_DATA

2) Sales generator stored procedures
- Main generator handler: snowpark/crocevia_sales_generator.py: run(session, start_date, end_date, overwrite)
- 20% stores variant: snowpark/crocevia_sales_generator_20pct.py: run(session, start_date, end_date, overwrite)
- SP returns (no STORE_TYPE): ORDER_ID, STORE_ID, STORE_NAME, SALE_DATE, PRODUCT_ID, QUANTITY, SALES_PRICE_EURO, DISCOUNT_AMOUNT_EURO, PAYMENT_METHOD, SALES_ASSISTANT_ID, CUSTOMER_ID
- Output tables:
  - Full: CROCEVIA_DB.BRONZE_DATA.CROCEVIA_SALES
  - 20% stores: CROCEVIA_DB.BRONZE_DATA.CROCEVIA_SALES_20PCT_STORES
- Always append to output (ignore overwrite flag for write mode); repeated CALLs accumulate rows

3) Business logic assumptions/decisions
- Target revenue: 25% of Carrefour France quarterly gross sales
- Daily target allocation: proportional to normalized daily CLOSE within each quarter
- Order totals: truncated normal distribution centered at ~€60, std ~€20; no discounts (DISCOUNT_AMOUNT_EURO = 0)
- Each order usually contains multiple line items (sample up to ~6), but can be single-item if target met
- ~40% of orders have CUSTOMER_ID attached (from CROCEVIA_DB.BRONZE_DATA.CROCEVIA_CRM)
- Store type is removed entirely (no weighting, no column)

4) Data quality handling
- Stock gaps: before weighting, fill missing dates in the requested window via linear interpolation of CLOSE
- Post-write verification: validate no missing SALE_DATE in the requested window; raise error if gaps
- Price parsing: robust EU price normalization (e.g., "1 299,00€" -> 1299.00) before using product prices

5) Performance/testing practices
- For faster tests, use the 20% stores SP: GENERATE_CROCEVIA_SALES_20PCT_STORES
- Progress prints enabled: sources loaded, stock window days, number of days targeted, per-day rows written, cumulative rows
- Register handlers via PUT to RAW stage; recreate SPs to rebind IMPORTS after updates

6) Naming and deployment
- Main SP name: GENERATE_CROCEVIA_SALES
- 20% stores SP name: GENERATE_CROCEVIA_SALES_20PCT_STORES
- IMPORTS path examples:
  - @CROCEVIA_DB.RAW_DATA.CR_BRONZE_STAGE/crocevia_sales_generator.py
  - @CROCEVIA_DB.RAW_DATA.CR_BRONZE_STAGE/crocevia_sales_generator_20pct.py

7) Append-only policy
- Generators must never overwrite output tables; writes are append mode to preserve history

8) Demo naming
- Current E2E demo codename: crocevia_c360

9) Crocevia C360 demo components
- Notebook: notebooks/crocevia_c360_audience_segmentation.ipynb
- Streamlit app: streamlit/customer_recommender.py
- Docs: README.md, docs/runbook.md, docs/changelog.md

10) Data validation workflow
- Before coding (and whenever debugging), use the relevant SnowCLI connection to inspect referenced Snowflake tables so notebook logic matches actual schemas

11) Learning capture
- After every new guideline or clarification (including Streamlit display requirements), append it here so the rulebook stays current

12) Snowflake notebook hygiene
- Default to Streamlit primitives (`st.write`, `st.dataframe`, etc.) for output in Snowsight notebooks

13) Schema verification
- Before coding in any project, run a SnowCLI describe/preview for each referenced table to align code with the actual schema

- When modeling in Snowpark ML, explicitly cast DECIMAL/NUMERIC features to DOUBLE before scaling/training to avoid implicit Decimal(38, 12) -> DoubleType conversion and precision warnings.

- For Snowpark ML model persistence, pass sample_input_data (e.g., 100 rows) to model.save to avoid auto schema inference on large datasets and ensure deterministic signatures.

- Modeling hygiene and join safety:
  - Verify join key uniqueness; deduplicate dimension/CRM tables before joins; guard for join explosions (row count vs distinct id).
  - Enforce one row per entity in features; drop duplicates by primary key before modeling.
  - Deduplicate predictions per entity before writes to GOLD.
  - Use dynamic schema introspection with column fallbacks; avoid hard-coded optional column names.
  - Segment sanity checks: ensure minimum cluster size; adjust n_clusters or merge tiny clusters if needed.
  - Always state the strictly necessary cells to rerun after edits.

14) SnowCLI + Programmatic Access Tokens (PAT) troubleshooting
- Snowflake CLI 3.3.0 may fail early with "251006: Password is empty" if a connection is configured with PAT-style fields but no password; Snowflake CLI 3.15.0 correctly attempts PAT auth when configured with authenticator="PROGRAMMATIC_ACCESS_TOKEN" and token_file_path.
- If SnowCLI returns "Programmatic access token is invalid" (250001/08001) even after rotating the token, the root cause is Snowflake-side (token/user/account mismatch, token revoked/rotated, PAT not allowed by authentication policy, or PAT network policy enforcement blocking the client IP). The same error can appear whether the token is supplied via PAT authenticator+token_file_path or passed as a password.
